3+2 under the machine frame kinematics, Fanuc's second feature coordinate
system, on a table machine (xyzac-trt-kins, table.ini) and a head machine
(5axiskins, head.ini).

The same program runs under the TCP kinematics and under the machine frame
kinematics (G13.1), from the same joints: a plane, G53.1, G53.6 or G68.3,
then moves and an arc in the plane, with no tool, a tool given before the
plane and a tool given in it.  The machine goes to the same joints every
time: under G13.1 the plane is on the part as the TCP kinematics places it,
with the tool along the tilted tool.

G53.6 under G13.1 keeps the tip on the part the whole way, not only at the
ends: status part_position stays on its point while the rotaries turn, and
the move ends where the TCP kinematics ends it, G13.1 still in force.  An
abort and a feed hold part way leave the tip on its point, and the machine
goes on from there to the same end.  A slide that swings past its limit
half way round, the tool turning from one side of the vertical to the
other, refuses G53.6 under G13.1 as under the TCP kinematics.

Then, under G13.1 only: a C word moves C alone and the frame stays where it
was taken (the slides do not move and a move to the same point stays put);
G68.4 with nothing in it after the turn lands where the TCP kinematics does
after the same turn; G53 goes to machine coordinates and G69 leaves plain
machine moves; a switch to the TCP kinematics and back in the plane keeps
the plane on the part.

Status keeps the plane as defined, the same under the TCP kinematics and
under G13.1, and the second feature coordinate system apart, the identity
except under G13.1; a plane X jog under G13.1 goes where G1 X+5 in the plane
does.

table.ini sets RETAIN_WORK_PLANE: a program that goes on to another plane is
aborted during its first move, and the plane the machine was in comes back
onto the part it was defined on.

Last the preview: after a plane set in MDI, with the tool in the spindle,
after a plane set in MDI and a rotary turned since, and with a plane in the
program itself, the preview's interpreter ends where the machine does.  What
the preview draws is on the part, as under the TCP kinematics: the same
program draws one plane in the same place, and ends its cut at the same
point, under G13.1 as under the TCP kinematics; G53.6 is drawn with the same
vertices under both, the tip held, not the loop the slides would make
running straight between the ends.  The live plot is on the
part too: under G13.1 in the plane it is where the TCP kinematics plots the
same point, with the tool frame in status the TCP kinematics', and switches
to the TCP kinematics and back neither move it nor draw a line.

With no plane, a rotary turned under G13.1 at a tilted head or table: the
preview draws the turn through the point the machine reaches half way and
ends where it ends, and the live plot and status part_position agree, the
TCP kinematics reading the same point from the same joints.  A switch to
the TCP kinematics and one to G13.1, at a tilt and with no plane: the
preview reads the point back from the joints as the machine does, and the
move after the switch ends where the machine ends it.
